@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@using MUI.Web.Data
@using MUI.Web.Localization
@inject CatalogueSource Catalogue

@* A connect screen is a figure a reader may want past in one keystroke, so the page needs somewhere
   for that keystroke to land. *@
<a class="skip" href="#content">@L("a11y.skipToContent")</a>

@*
    A site whose whole claim is that its data is measured may not show invented data silently. This
    is deliberately not dismissible and not subtle: a reader who cannot tell demo data from a
    measurement is being misled by exactly the mechanism this project exists to replace.
*@
@if (!Catalogue.IsMeasured)
{
    <p class="demo-banner" role="status">
        <strong>@L("banner.demo.lead")</strong> @L("banner.demo")
    </p>
}

@*
    Nine links in one flat row, in no order anybody could name, and no mark on the one you were
    reading. They are two ideas and two odd ones out: games · archive · find · random are places to
    browse, reference · ecosystem · rankings are things to read about the hobby — and submit is an
    action rather than a category, about is site meta. The first two are labelled groups, the last
    two moved to the far end beside sign in, where a reader looks for what to *do*.
*@
<div class="shell">
<header class="site">
    <a class="mark" href="@HttpContext.Link("/")">mu<span class="star">*</span>index</a>
    @*
        The bar degrades through a defined order rather than through whatever flexbox decides
        (i18n S2): group labels first, then submit's border, then both groups into one disclosure.
        Width triggers each step, never language — a German reader on a wide monitor never leaves
        step 0 and an English reader in a narrow window reaches step 2.

        Every destination survives all three steps. That is what makes the disclosure a `details`
        holding the same seven links rather than a shorter bar: a step that dropped a link would be
        a navigation that changes what the site contains depending on the window.
    *@
    @*
        **Every link exists exactly once.** Step 3 is a disclosure wrapping the same two groups, not
        a second copy of them beside the first — a duplicate set would put two `aria-current="page"`
        markers in one document and hand a screen reader the catalogue twice. Above the collapse
        width the summary is hidden and the disclosure's content is forced visible, so one markup is
        a bar on a laptop and a menu on a phone with no script and no second rendering.
    *@
    <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="@L("nav.catalogues")">
        <details class="nav-menu">
            <summary>@L("nav.menu") <span aria-hidden="true">▾</span></summary>
            <div class="menu-panel">
                <span class="nav-group" role="group" aria-label="@L("nav.browse")">
                    <span class="nav-label" aria-hidden="true">@L("nav.browse")</span>
                    @Item("/games", L("nav.games"))
                    @Item("/find", L("nav.find"))
                    @Item("/games/random", L("nav.random"))
                    @Item("/archive", L("nav.archive"))
                </span>
                <span class="nav-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <span class="nav-group" role="group" aria-label="@L("nav.learn")">
                    <span class="nav-label" aria-hidden="true">@L("nav.learn")</span>
                    @Item("/reference", L("nav.reference"))
                    @Item("/ecosystem", L("nav.ecosystem"))
                    @Item("/rankings", L("nav.rankings"))
                </span>

                @*
                    Step 4. On a phone the right cluster runs out of room too, so submit comes in
                    here rather than being dropped — the rule is still that a step loses decoration
                    and never a destination. About and sign in stay in the bar: the first is two
                    words, and the second is the only item at that end that changes what the site
                    will let a reader do.
                *@
                <span class="menu-tail">
                    <span class="nav-label" aria-hidden="true">@L("nav.thisSite")</span>
                    <a href="@HttpContext.Link("/submit")">@L("nav.submitGame")</a>
                </span>

                @*
                    Step 5. The two reading controls, at the width where the bar has no room left
                    for them — the same move `submit` makes one step earlier, for the same reason.
                    The theme control stays in the bar at every width: it is three short words and
                    the ladder already spends its type size on them.

                    Rendered here *and* in the bar, with CSS showing exactly one, which is what
                    `menu-tail` above already does. The alternative — one copy moved by CSS — is not
                    available: the panel and the trailing cluster are different flex containers and
                    nothing moves a box between two of those. The switcher carries no `id` for that
                    reason; see LanguageSwitcher on the label that used to need one.
                *@
                <span class="menu-reading">
                    <span class="nav-label" aria-hidden="true">@L("nav.reading")</span>
                    @Mirror
                    <LanguageSwitcher />
                </span>
            </div>
        </details>
    </nav>
    @*
        The account, in a slot of its own rather than as a tenth entry above — that list names places
        to browse and an operator's own page is not one of them. Last, so it sits at the far end of
        the bar where a reader looks for it.

        It was missing entirely, and everything §8.5 grants was behind it: the enrichment fields, the
        connect-screen decision, the opt-out. All of them rendered, tested and reachable only by
        somebody who remembered to type /account.
    *@
    <nav class="account" aria-label="@L("nav.account")">
        @Item("/about", L("nav.about"))
        <a class="action" href="@HttpContext.Link("/submit")">@L("nav.submit")</a>
        @if (HttpContext?.User is { Identity.IsAuthenticated: true })
        {
            <a class="action primary" href="@HttpContext.Link("/account")">@L("nav.yourGames")</a>
        }
        else
        {
            <a class="action primary" href="@HttpContext.Link("/account/sign-in")">@L("nav.signIn")</a>
        }
    </nav>
    @*
        Last in the bar, because these are the controls that do not go anywhere: everything to their
        left names a page, and these three change the one you are on — as text, in another language,
        against another background.

        They used to be a footer. It said "all games · archive · submit · plain text" under a bar
        that already carried the first three, so on every page the last thing a reader met was the
        navigation they had already been given, and the two controls that were *not* duplicates were
        buried at the end of it. The duplicates are gone and the two survivors came up here.
    *@
    <div class="reading">
        @Mirror
        <LanguageSwitcher />
        <ThemeToggle />
    </div>
</header>
<main id="content">@Body</main>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// One nav item, marked when it is the page being read.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// <c>aria-current="page"</c> is the fact; the underline is the drawing of it. The marker is an
    /// inset box-shadow rather than a border or a padding change, because either of those adds a
    /// pixel to the item's box and shifts every link beside it the moment it becomes current — a
    /// bar that moves when you arrive is a bar you cannot aim at.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// A section matches its own root and everything under it, so a reference article marks
    /// "reference" — but "/" matches only itself, or every item on the site would be current on the
    /// home page.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <b>The item is named by the path it has in every language and written in the one this page
    /// is being read in.</b> Those are two different strings and only the first can be compared
    /// against the request: the middleware moves the locale into <c>PathBase</c>, so
    /// <c>Request.Path</c> on <c>/de/games</c> is <c>/games</c> — and a nav that marked the current
    /// page by its written form would mark nothing at all outside English.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private RenderFragment Item(string href, string text) =>
        @<a href="@HttpContext.Link(href)" class="@(IsCurrent(href) ? "on" : null)"
            aria-current="@(IsCurrent(href) ? "page" : null)">@text</a>;

    /// <summary>
    /// The offer to read this page as text, where there is one.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// Conditional, because six of the site's routes have no text mirror and a bar that offered one
    /// on all of them would be advertising a link that answers with the page you are already on.
    /// <see cref="TextMirror"/> holds which are which, and why the page cannot be the one to say so.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The href carries the query. Four of the eleven footers this replaces wrote a bare
    /// <c>?plain=1</c>, so asking a filtered listing for text threw the filter away and answered
    /// with the whole catalogue.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private RenderFragment Mirror =>
        TextMirror.Offers(HttpContext)
            ? @<a class="mirror" href="@TextMirror.Href(HttpContext)">@L("a11y.plainText")</a>
            : @<text></text>;

    /// <summary>
    /// Every destination this bar offers, longest first, so the most specific match wins.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>Two items were marked at once.</b> A section matches its own root and everything under it,
    /// which is what makes a reference article mark "reference" — but <c>/games/random</c> is its own
    /// destination in the same bar, so standing on it matched <c>/games</c> as well and a screen
    /// reader was handed two "current page" markers in one document. `aria-current` names the page
    /// you are on, and there is one of those.
    /// </remarks>
    private static readonly string[] Destinations =
    [
        .. new[] { "/games/random", "/games", "/find", "/archive", "/reference", "/ecosystem", "/rankings", "/about", "/" }
            .OrderByDescending(d => d.Length),
    ];

    private bool IsCurrent(string href)
    {
        var path = HttpContext?.Request.Path.Value?.TrimEnd('/');

        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(path))
        {
            path = "/";
        }

        // The best match, not merely a match: the first destination this path sits under is the one
        // that gets the marker, and every shorter one that also contains it gets nothing.
        var best = Destinations.FirstOrDefault(d => Matches(d, path));

        return best is not null && best == href;

        static bool Matches(string destination, string path) => destination == "/"
            ? path == "/"
            : path == destination || path.StartsWith(destination + "/", StringComparison.Ordinal);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The request, for the one question this layout asks it: is anybody signed in.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Every page on the site renders this header, so anything read here is read once per page view
    /// for ever. The operator's display name would be pleasant and would cost a query against the
    /// user store on the listing, the rankings and every game page to render four words — so the
    /// link says where it goes rather than who is holding it, and the dashboard does the greeting.
    /// </remarks>
    [CascadingParameter]
    private HttpContext? HttpContext { get; set; }

    /// <summary>One message, in the locale this request is being answered in.</summary>
    private string L(string id) => Messages.For(HttpContext.LocaleOf().Tag, id);
}
